<section xml:id="sec_integration-by-parts_sec_integration-by-parts">
  <title>Integration by Parts</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
          <idx><h>integration by parts</h></idx>
      As in the previous section,
    </p>
    <aside>
      <p>
        An integral form of the product rule
      </p>
    </aside>
    <p>
      suppose we have functions <m>F</m> and <m>G</m>,
      with corresponding derivatives <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>.
      If we use the product rule
          <idx><h>product rule</h></idx>
      to differentiate <m>F(x) \cdot G(x)</m>, we get:
      <me>
        \left( F \cdot G \right)' = F \cdot G' + F' \cdot G = F\cdot g + f\cdot G
      </me>.
    </p>
    <p>
      We can turn this into a statement about indefinite integrals<idx><h>integral</h><h>indefinite</h></idx>
      :
          <idx><h>indefinite integral</h></idx>
      <me>
        \int \left( F \cdot g + f \cdot G \right) \, dx = \int \left( F \cdot G \right)' \, dx = F(x) \cdot G(x) + C
      </me>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unfortunately,
      in this form the statement is not especially useful;
      it applies only when the integrand has two terms of the special form <m>f \cdot g' + f' \cdot g</m>.
      However, if we rewrite the statement in the form
    </p>
    <image source=""/>
    <p>
      it becomes very useful.
      <term>Example 1</term>We will use the formula in the box to find
      <me>
        \int x \cdot \cos{x} \, dx
      </me>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If we label the parts of this integrand as follows:
      <me>
        F(x) = x \qquad \qquad g(x) = \cos{x}
      </me>,
      then we have
      <me>
        f(x) = 1 \qquad \mbox{and}  \qquad G(x) = \sin{x}
      </me>.
    </p>
    <p>
      According to the formula,
      <md>
        <mrow>\int x \cdot \cos{x} \, dx \amp = x \cdot \sin{x} - \int \sin{x} \, dx</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = x \cdot \sin{x} + \cos{x} + C</mrow>
      </md>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The integrand is first broken into two parts<mdash/>in this case,
    </p>
    <aside>
      <p>
        Integrate only part of the integrand
      </p>
    </aside>
    <p>
      <m>x</m> and <m>\cos{x}</m>.
      One part is differentiated while the other part is integrated. (The part we integrated is <m>g(x) = \cos{x}</m>,
      and we got <m>G(x) = \sin{x}</m>.) For this reason,
      the rule described in the box is called
      <term>integration by parts</term>.
          <idx><h>integration by parts</h></idx>
      As with integration by substitution,
      integration by parts exchanges one integration task for another: Instead of finding an antiderivative for <m>F \cdot g</m>,
      we must find one for <m>f \cdot G</m>.
      The idea is to
      <q>trade-in</q>
      one integration problem for a more readily solvable one.
      <term>Example 2</term>Use integration by parts to find
      <me>
        \int \ln(x) \, dx
      </me>.
    </p>
    <p>
      At first glance we can't integrate by parts,
      because there aren't two parts!
      But note that we can write
      <me>
        \ln(x) = \ln(x) \cdot 1
      </me>,
      and then set
      <me>
        F(x) = \ln(x), \qquad \qquad g(x) = 1
      </me>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This implies
      <me>
        f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x} \qquad \mbox{and}  \qquad G(x) = x
      </me>,
      and the integration by parts formula now gives us
      <md>
        <mrow>\int \ln(x) \, dx \amp = x \cdot \ln(x) - \int x \cdot \dfrac{1}{x} \, dx</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = x \cdot \ln(x) - \int 1 \, dx</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = x \cdot \ln(x) - x + C</mrow>
      </md>.
    </p>
    <p>
      We thus see that integration by parts
          <idx><h>integration by parts</h></idx>
      <mdash/>like integration by substitution<mdash/>is an art rather than a set routine.
    </p>
    <aside>
      <p>
        The ingredients of a successful integration by parts
      </p>
    </aside>
    <p>
      If integration by parts is to work,
      several things must happen.
      First, you need to see that the method might actually apply.
      (In Example 2 this wasn't obvious.)
      Next, you need to identify the parts of the integrand that will be differentiated and integrated,
      respectively.
      The wrong choices can lead you away from a solution,
      rather than towards one. (See example 3 below for a cautionary tale.
          <idx><h>integration by parts</h><h>cautionary tale</h></idx>
      ) Finally, you need to be able to carry out the integration of the new integral <m>\int f \cdot G \, dx</m>.
      As you work you may have to reshape the integrand algebraically.
      Technique comes with practice, and luck is useful, too.
      <term>Example 3</term>Use integration by parts to find
      <me>
        \int t \cdot e^t \, dt
      </me>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Set
      <me>
        F(t) = e^t, \qquad \mbox \qquad g(t) = t;
      </me>
      then
      <me>
        f(t) = e^t, \qquad \mbox \qquad G(t) = \dfrac{t^2}{2}
      </me>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The integration by parts formula then gives
      <me>
        \int t \cdot e^t \, dt = \dfrac{t^2}{2} e^t - \int \dfrac{t^2}{2} e^t\,dt
      </me>.
    </p>
    <p>
      While this is a true statement,
      we are not better off<mdash/>the new integral is <em>not</em>
      simpler than the original.
    </p>
    <aside>
      <p>
        What went wrong?
      </p>
    </aside>
    <p>
      A solution is eluding us here.
      You will have a chance to do this problem properly in the exercises.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="sec_integration-by-parts_subsec_exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>
    <introduction>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use integration by parts to find a formula for each of the following integrals.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int x \sin{x}\,dx}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int te^{t}\,dt}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int we^{-w}\,dw}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int x\ln{x}\,dx}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int \arcsin{x}\,dx}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int \arctan{x}\,dx}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int x^2e^{-x}\,dx}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dint u^2 \cos{u} \, du</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int x\sec^2{x}\,dx}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int e^{2x}(x + e^x)\,dx}</m>(Suggestion for part (g): Apply integration by parts twice.
                  After the first application you should have an integral that can itself be evaluated using integration by parts.)
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use integration by parts to obtain a formula for
            <me>
              \int (\ln{x})^2\,dx
            </me>.
            Choose <m>f = \ln{x}</m> and also <m>g' = \ln{x}</m>.
            To continue you need to find <m>g</m>,
            the antiderivative of <m>\ln{x}</m>,
            but this has already been obtained in the text.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find <m>\dint x^2 e^x \, dx</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Find <m>\dint x^3 e^x \, dx</m>. (Reduce this to part (a)).
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Find <m>\dint x^4 e^x \, dx</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What is the general pattern here?
                  Find a formula for <m>\dint x^n e^x \, dx</m>,
                  where <m>n</m> is any positive integer.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Find <m>\dint e^x \left(5x^2 - 3x + 7\right) \, dx</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Draw the graph of <m>y=\arctan{x}</m> over the interval <m>0\leq x\leq 1</m>.
            You could have gotten the same graph by thinking of <m>x</m> as a function of <m>y</m><mdash/>write down this relationship and the corresponding <m>y</m> interval.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Evaluate
                  <me>
                    \int_0^1 \arctan{x}\,dx
                  </me>
                  and show on your graph the area this corresponds to.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Evaluate
                  <me>
                    \int_0^{\pi/4} \tan{y}\,dy
                  </me>
                  and show on your graph the area this corresponds to.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  If we add the results of part (b) and part (c), what do you get?
                  From the geometry of the picture,
                  what should you have gotten?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Repeat the analysis of the preceding problem by calculating the value of
            <me>
              \int_0^2 x^3\,dx+\int_0^8 y^{1/3}\,dy
            </me>,
            and seeing if it agrees with what you would predict by looking at the graphs.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Generalize the preceding two problems to the case where <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are any two functions that are inverses of each other whose graphs pass through the origin.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            What is the average value of the function <m>\ln{x}</m> on the interval <m>[1, e]</m>?
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What is the average value of <m>\ln{x}</m> on <m>[1, b]</m>?
                  Express this in terms of <m>b</m>.
                  Discuss the following claim: The average value of <m>\ln{x}</m> on <m>[1, b]</m> is approximately
                  <m>\ln(b) - 1</m> when <m>b</m> is large.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Sketch the graph of <m>f(x) = x e^{-x}</m> on the interval <m>[0, 4]</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What is the area between the graph of
                  <m>y = f(x)</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis for <m>0 \leq x \leq 4</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What is the area between the graph of
                  <m>y = f(x)</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis for <m>0 \leq x \leq b</m>?
                  Express your answer in terms of <m>b</m>,
                  and denote it <m>A(b)</m>.
                  What is <m>A(100)</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find three solutions <m>y = f(t)</m> to the differential equation
            <me>
              \dfrac{dy}{dt} = 5 - 2 \ln{t}
            </me>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the solution <m>y = \varphi(t)</m> to the initial value problem
            <me>
              \dfrac{dy}{dt} = t e^{-t^2/2}, \qquad y(0) = 2
            </me>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The function <m>\varphi(t)</m> increases as <m>t</m> increases.
                  Show this first by sketching the graph of <m>y = \varphi(t)</m>.
                  Show it also by referring to the differential equation that
                  <m>\varphi(t)</m> satisfies. (What is true about the derivative of an increasing function?)
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Does the value of <m>\varphi(t)</m> increase without bound as <m>t \to \infty</m>?
                  If not, what value does <m>\varphi(t)</m> approach?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>The differential form of Integration by Parts</term><nbsp/><nbsp/> If <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are expressions in <m>x</m>,
            then the product rule can be written as
            <me>
              \dfrac{d}{dx}(u\cdot v) = \dfrac{du}{dx}\cdot v + u \cdot \dfrac{dv}{dx}
            </me>.
            Explain carefully how this leads to the following statement of integration by parts,
            and why it is equivalent to the form in the text:
            <me>
              \int u\,dv = uv-\int v\,du
            </me>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Solve a couple of the preceding problems using this notation.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </introduction>
    <subsubsection xml:id="sec_integration-by-parts_subsubsec_sine-and-cosine-integrals">
      <title>Sine and cosine integrals</title>
      <p>
            <idx><h>integral</h><h>sine and cosine</h></idx>
        The purpose of the remaining exercises is to establish integral formulas that we will use to analyze Fourier polynomials and the power spectrum in chapter<nbsp/>12.
        In the first three, <m>\alpha</m> is a constant:
        <md>
          <mrow>\int \sin^2{\alpha x}\,dx \amp = \dfrac{x}{2} - \dfrac{1}{4\alpha}\sin{2\alpha x} + C,</mrow>
          <mrow>\int \cos^2{\alpha x}\,dx \amp = \dfrac{x}{2} + \dfrac{1}{4\alpha}\sin{2\alpha x} + C,</mrow>
          <mrow>\int \sin{\alpha x}\cos{\alpha x}\,dx \amp = - \dfrac{1}{4\alpha}\cos{2\alpha x} + C</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the remaining four, <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\beta</m> are
        <em>different</em> constants:
        <md>
          <mrow>\int \sin{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x}\,dx \amp = \dfrac{1}{\beta^2-\alpha^2} \left(\alpha\cos{\alpha x}\sin{\beta x} - \beta\sin{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}\right) + C,</mrow>
          <mrow>\int \cos{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}\,dx \amp = \dfrac{1}{\beta^2-\alpha^2} \left(\beta\cos{\alpha x}\sin{\beta x} - \alpha\sin{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}\right) + C,</mrow>
          <mrow>\int \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}\,dx \amp = \dfrac{1}{\beta^2-\alpha^2} \left(\beta\sin{\alpha x}\sin{\beta x} + \alpha\cos{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}\right) + C,</mrow>
          <mrow>\int \cos{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x}\,dx \amp =  \dfrac{1}{\beta^2-\alpha^2} \left(-\alpha\sin{\alpha x}\sin{\beta x} - \beta\cos{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}\right) + C</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li xml:id="sec_integration-by-parts_x_trig_">
          <p>
            In the later exercises we shall make frequent use of the following
            <q>trigonometric identities</q>:
    <idx><h>trigonometric identities</h></idx>
            <md>
              <mrow>2 \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\alpha x} \amp = \sin{2\alpha x},</mrow>
              <mrow>\cos^2{\alpha x} - \sin^2{\alpha x} \amp =  \cos{2\alpha x},</mrow>
              <mrow>\sin^2{\alpha x} + \cos^2{\alpha x} \amp = 1</mrow>
            </md>.
            Using a graphing package on a computer,
            graph together the functions
            <me>
              2 \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\alpha x} \qquad \mbox{and} \qquad \sin{2\alpha x}
            </me>
            to show that they seem to be identical.  (That is, show that they
            <q>share phosphor.</q>
            )  Then do the same for the pairs of functions in the other two identities.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                We can give a different argument for the identities above using the ideas we have developed in studying initial value problems.
                To prove the first identity, for instance,
                let <m>f(x) = 2 \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\alpha x}</m>,
                and let <m>g(x) = \sin{2\alpha x}</m>.
                Show that both functions satisfy
                <me>
                  y'' = -4\alpha^2 y, \mbox{with} y(0)=0\mbox{and}  y'(0)=2\alpha
                </me>.
                Hence conclude the two functions must be the same.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find an initial value problem that is satisfied by both
                <m>f(x)=\cos^2{\alpha x} - \sin^2{\alpha x}</m> and by <m>g(x) = \cos{2\alpha x}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Evaluating </term> <m>\displaystyle \int \sin^2{x} \, dx</m>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Using integration by parts, show that
                  <me>
                    \int \sin^2{x} \, dx = - \sin{x} \cos{x} + \int \cos^2{x} \, dx
                  </me>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Using the identity <m>\sin^2{\alpha x} + \cos^2{\alpha x} = 1</m>,
                  show that the new integral can be written as
                  <me>
                    x - \int \sin^2{x} \, dx
                  </me>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Combining (a) and (b) algebraically, show that
                  <me>
                    2 \int \sin^2{x} \, dx = - \sin{x} \cos{x} + x + C
                  </me>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Using algebra and a trigonometric identity, conclude that
                  <me>
                    \int \sin^2{x} \, dx = \dfrac{x}{2} - \dfrac{1}{4} \sin{2x} + C
                  </me>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Modify the argument of the preceding exercise to show
            <me>
              \int \sin^2{\alpha x}\,dx  = \dfrac{x}{2} - \dfrac{1}{4\alpha}\sin{2\alpha x} + C
            </me>.
            and
            <me>
              \int \cos^2{\alpha x}\,dx  = \dfrac{x}{2} + \dfrac{1}{4\alpha}\sin{2\alpha x} + C
            </me>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Evaluating </term><m>\displaystyle \int \sin^2{\alpha x} \, dx</m> Determine this integral anew,
            without using integration by parts,
            by carrying out the following steps.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  From the trigonometric identities<idx><h>trigonometric identities</h></idx> <xref ref="sec_integration-by-parts_x_trig_">on</xref>,
                  deduce that
                  <me>
                    2 \sin^2 {\alpha x} = 1 - \cos{2 \alpha x}
                  </me>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Using the formula in (a), conclude that
                  <me>
                    \int \sin^2{\alpha x} \, dx = \dfrac{x}{2} - \dfrac{1}{4\alpha}\sin{2\alpha x} + C
                  </me>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Evaluating </term><m>\displaystyle \int \cos^2{\alpha x} \, dx</m> Using only algebra and the identity <m>\sin^2{\alpha x} + \cos^2{\alpha x} = 1</m>,
            show that the previous exercise implies
            <me>
              \int \cos^2{\alpha x} \, dx = \dfrac{x}{2} + \dfrac{1}{4\alpha}\sin{2\alpha x} + C
            </me>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Evaluating </term><m>\displaystyle \int \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\alpha x} \, dx</m>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Using the identity <m>\sin{2\alpha x} = 2 \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\alpha x}</m>,
                  deduce the following formula
                  <me>
                    \int \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\alpha x} \, dx = -\dfrac{1}{4\alpha} \cos{ 2 \alpha x} + C
                  </me>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Using integration by substitution,
                  obtain the alternative formula
                  <me>
                    \int \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\alpha x} \, dx = \dfrac{1}{2\alpha} \sin^2{\alpha x} + C
                  </me>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Show that your results in (a) and (b) are compatible. (For example,
                  use exercise 15<nbsp/>(a).)
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Evaluating </term><m>{\displaystyle \int \sin{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x}\, dx}</m>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Use integration by parts to show that
                  <me>
                    \int \sin{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x}\, dx = -\dfrac{1}{\alpha} \cos{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x} + \dfrac{\beta}{\alpha} \int \cos{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}\, dx
                  </me>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Using integration by parts again,
                  show that the new integral in part (a) can be written as
                  <me>
                    \int \cos{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}\, dx = \dfrac{1}{\alpha} \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x} + \dfrac{\beta}{\alpha} \int \sin{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x}\, dx
                  </me>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Let <m>J = \dint \sin{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x}\, dx</m>;
                  show that combining (a) and (b) gives
                  <me>
                    J = -\dfrac{1}{\alpha} \cos{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x} + \dfrac{\beta}{\alpha^2} \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x} + \dfrac{\beta^2}{\alpha^2} J
                  </me>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Solve (c) for <m>J</m> to find
                  <me>
                    \int \sin{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x}\, dx = \dfrac{1}{\beta^2 - \alpha^2} (\alpha \cos{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x} - \beta \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}) + C
                  </me>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Imitate the methods of the preceding exercise to deduce
            <me>
              \int \cos{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}\, dx = \dfrac{1}{\beta^2 - \alpha^2} (\beta \cos{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x} - \alpha \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}) + C
            </me>
            and
            <me>
              \int \sin{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}\, dx = \dfrac{1}{\beta^2 - \alpha^2} (\beta \sin{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x} + \alpha \cos{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}) + C
            </me>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Evaluating </term><m>{\displaystyle \int \cos{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x}\, dx}</m> This integral is the same as one in the preceding exercise,
            if you exchange the factors <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\beta</m>.
            Do that, and obtain the formula
            <me>
              \int \cos{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x}\, dx = {1}{\alpha^2 - \beta^2} (\alpha \sin{\alpha x} \sin{\beta x} + \beta \cos{\alpha x} \cos{\beta x}) +C
            </me>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Determine the following.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int_0^{2\pi} \sin^2{x}\, dx}</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int_0^{n\pi} \cos^2{x}\, dx}</m>,
                  <m>n</m> a positive integer.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int_0^{2\pi} \sin{x} \sin{\beta x}\, dx}</m>,
                  <m>\beta \neq 1</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>{\displaystyle \int_0^{\pi} \sin{x} \sin{\beta x}\, dx}</m>,
                  <m>\beta \neq 1</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>
</section>